Vitiligo (Mild Acrofacial)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Loss of pigment around mouth/eyes or fingertips, stable over long periods

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-049
Reason for Visit: Acute severe psychological distress and panic upon discovering new depigmentation, requiring exclusion of a severe fungal infection or chemical burn.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate the sudden discovery of a stark white, altered patch of skin on the face. Immediate clinical review was strictly necessary to rule out an acute chemical leukoderma, severe superficial fungal infection (e.g., Pityriasis Versicolor), or evolving skin necrosis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I looked in the mirror and the skin around my mouth has gone completely white, like a ghost. I'm terrified it's a flesh-eating fungus or I've been burned by something.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports depigmentation limited to distal extremities and facial orifices. While removing makeup tonight, the patient noticed a distinct, chalk-white macule at the corner of the mouth that was likely present but obscured previously. The sudden discovery triggered immense anxiety, frantic scrubbing of the area (causing reactive redness), and a fear of aggressive infection or permanent facial disfigurement. The lesion is completely painless and non-pruritic. Denies application of strong chemicals or bleaches.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High aesthetic distress and acute anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, highly distressed regarding facial appearance, holding a mirror.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals a distinct, well-demarcated, chalk-white macule at the right oral commissure. The skin texture is entirely normal; there is absolutely no scaling, induration, atrophy, or ulceration. A faint rim of blanching erythema is present, consistent with the patient's reported vigorous scrubbing. Loss of pigment around mouth/eyes or fingertips, stable over long periods (patient now notes a similar small white spot on a fingertip upon inspection).
Environmental Observations: Bathroom counter has various normal cosmetics; no industrial chemicals.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current topical medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vitiligo (Mild Acrofacial) with secondary health anxiety - Status: Stable cutaneous finding
Differentials:

Pityriasis Versicolor - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fine, powdery scale on the lesion, and classic chalk-white depigmentation rather than hypopigmented (faintly lighter) macules.

Chemical Leukoderma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Negative history of exposure to potent phenolic compounds or strong bleaching agents, and the specific autoimmune acrofacial pattern (mouth/fingertips).

Cutaneous Necrosis/Ischemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal capillary refill in the surrounding tissue, lack of pain, and completely intact skin barrier.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe psychological distress secondary to discovering a stark white facial macule, heavily fearing an infectious or necrotic process. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to rule out acute chemical injury or spreading infection. Bedside evaluation definitively established the lesion as a classic, benign vitiligo macule—entirely lacking scale, pain, or tissue destruction. Providing immediate clinical reassurance to halt the patient's mechanical scrubbing of the face and de-escalating the severe aesthetic panic was strictly necessary, preventing an inappropriate, highly distressed emergency department presentation tonight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, non-infectious, and non-painful nature of the condition. Instructed the patient to cease all rubbing and scrubbing of the area.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a plain moisturizer to the area and completely avoid looking in the mirror for the rest of the night. By stopping the frantic scrubbing, the red, irritated ring will vanish by morning, proving to you that the skin is healthy and simply lacking pigment, not infected.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP or dermatology appointment in 2-3 weeks to discuss potential treatments (e.g., topical calcineurin inhibitors) or camouflage techniques.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the white patch breaks open into a bleeding ulcer, if it becomes extremely hot and painful to the touch, or if you develop a severe, spreading rash accompanied by a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Vitiligo is a purely cosmetic condition where the immune system clears away the pigment cells in small areas. It does not cause pain, it is not contagious, and the skin remains structurally perfectly healthy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the patch develops a thick, crusty scale, or if it begins to rapidly spread down your neck over a few days.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The white spot is entirely harmless. The redness around it is only there because you scrubbed it too hard in a panic. There is no infection to fight.
Actionable Steps: Stop rubbing the area. Because the skin there has no melanin, it will burn easily in the sun; you must apply sunscreen to it daily. Your GP can discuss creams that may help bring the color back.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Vitiligo (benign loss of skin pigment). Exam shows NO infection and NO skin damage. Stop rubbing the area. Safe to rest. See GP routinely.

